# Configuration file for dircolors, a utility to help you set the
# LS_COLORS environment variable used by GNU ls with the --color option.

# Copyright (C) 1996-2023 Free Software Foundation, Inc.
# Copying and distribution of this file, with or without modification,
# are permitted provided the copyright notice and this notice are preserved.

#
# The keywords COLOR, OPTIONS, and EIGHTBIT (honored by the
# slackware version of dircolors) are recognized but ignored.

# Global config options can be specified before TERM or COLORTERM entries

# ===================================================================
# Terminal filters
# ===================================================================
# Below are TERM or COLORTERM entries, which can be glob patterns, which
# restrict following config to systems with matching environment variables.
COLORTERM ?*
TERM Eterm
TERM ansi
TERM *color*
TERM con[0-9]*x[0-9]*
TERM cons25
TERM console
TERM cygwin
TERM *direct*
TERM dtterm
TERM gnome
TERM hurd
TERM jfbterm
TERM konsole
TERM kterm
TERM linux
TERM linux-c
TERM mlterm
TERM putty
TERM rxvt*
TERM screen*
TERM st
TERM terminator
TERM tmux*
TERM vt48;2;117;113;94
TERM xterm*
TERM wezterm

# ===================================================================
# Basic file attributes
# ===================================================================
# Below are the color init strings for the basic file types.
# One can use codes for 256 or more colors supported by modern terminals.
# The default color codes use the capabilities of an 8 color terminal
# with some additional attributes as per the following codes:
# Attribute codes:
# 00=none 01=bold 04=underscore 05=blink 07=reverse 08=concealed
# Text color codes:
# 38;2;39;40;34=black 38;2;249;38;114=red 38;2;166;226;46=green 38;2;230;219;116=yellow 38;2;102;217;239=blue 38;2;249;38;114=magenta 38;2;161;239;228=cyan 38;2;248;248;242=white
# Background color codes:
# 48;2;39;40;34=black 48;2;249;38;114=red 48;2;166;226;46=green 48;2;230;219;116=yellow 48;2;102;217;239=blue 48;2;249;38;114=magenta 48;2;161;239;228=cyan 48;2;248;248;242=white
#NORMAL 00	# no color code at all
#FILE 00	# regular file: use no color at all
RESET 0		# reset to "normal" color
DIR 01;38;2;102;217;239	# directory
LINK 01;38;2;161;239;228	# symbolic link.  (If you set this to 'target' instead of a
                # numerical value, the color is as for the file pointed to.)
MULTIHARDLINK 00	# regular file with more than one link
FIFO 48;2;39;40;34;38;2;230;219;116	# pipe
SOCK 01;38;2;249;38;114	# socket
DOOR 01;38;2;249;38;114	# door
BLK 48;2;39;40;34;38;2;230;219;116;01	# block device driver
CHR 48;2;39;40;34;38;2;230;219;116;01	# character device driver
ORPHAN 48;2;39;40;34;38;2;249;38;114;01 # symlink to nonexistent file, or non-stat'able file ...
MISSING 00      # ... and the files they point to
SETUID 38;2;248;248;242;48;2;249;38;114	# file that is setuid (u+s)
SETGID 38;2;39;40;34;48;2;230;219;116	# file that is setgid (g+s)
CAPABILITY 00	# file with capability (very expensive to lookup)
STICKY_OTHER_WRITABLE 38;2;39;40;34;48;2;166;226;46 # dir that is sticky and other-writable (+t,o+w)
OTHER_WRITABLE 38;2;102;217;239;48;2;166;226;46 # dir that is other-writable (o+w) and not sticky
STICKY 38;2;248;248;242;48;2;102;217;239	# dir with the sticky bit set (+t) and not other-writable

# This is for files with execute permission:
EXEC 01;38;2;166;226;46

# ===================================================================
# File extension attributes
# ===================================================================
# List any file extensions like '.gz' or '.tar' that you would like ls
# to color below. Put the suffix, a space, and the color init string.
# (and any comments you want to add after a '#').
# Suffixes are matched case insensitively, but if you define different
# init strings for separate cases, those will be honored.
#

# If you use DOS-style suffixes, you may want to uncomment the following:
#.cmd 01;38;2;166;226;46 # executables (bright green)
#.exe 01;38;2;166;226;46
#.com 01;38;2;166;226;46
#.btm 01;38;2;166;226;46
#.bat 01;38;2;166;226;46
# Or if you want to color scripts even if they do not have the
# executable bit actually set.
#.sh  01;38;2;166;226;46
#.csh 01;38;2;166;226;46

